      "problem": "Express $\\sqrt{\\frac{m+10}{m-10}}\\,$ without radicals in the denominator.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{m+10}{m-10}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{m+10}}{\\sqrt{m-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the radical $\\sqrt{m-10}$ from the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{m-10}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{m+10}}{\\sqrt{m-10}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{m+10}}{\\sqrt{m-10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{m-10}}{\\sqrt{m-10}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{m+10}\\cdot \\sqrt{m-10}}{\\sqrt{m-10}\\cdot \\sqrt{m-10}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(m+10)(m-10)}}{(\\sqrt{m-10})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{{m}^{2}-{10}^{2}}}{m-10} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{{m}^{2}-100}}{m-10}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
